Revision history for Database-BI

0.022.0	Mon Nov  9 10:33:59 AM EST 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- American English: a browser asking for en-US gets the pages in American
	  spelling (templates/web/en-us/); language => 'en-us' makes it the
	  default.
	- Messages from the server follow the same language: the browser's for
	  pages and replies, the locale (LANG) for the scripts.
	- A table or pivot of more than one page says how many there are
	  ("Page 2 of 7").  A SQLite table is counted once, and again only
	  when the file changes.
	- Rows per page: a list under a long table or pivot (25, 50, 100, 200,
	  All), and a default for it on the Preferences page.  per_page=0 is
	  every row.
	- The line graph can draw a trend line (least squares), with its slope
	  and R squared, beside Min, Avg and Max.
	- The Preferences page's breadcrumb leads back to the page it was
	  opened from.
	- Map places found online, extracts, the page cache and a table cache on
	  disc are kept where Object::Configure's cache_dir, CACHEDIR, CACHE_DIR
	  or cache_dir in database_bi.conf says, else where they were.
	[ Bug fixes ]
	- A number too large for the browser (1e999) is not a number on a perl
	  built with long doubles either (GitHub #6).
	- Sorting a table of more than one page sorts every row, so page 1 is the
	  start of the sorted table; it sorted only the rows on the page.
	- A code with digits in it (T27, CT4) sorts as text, not as its digits;
	  totals and charts on the page read numbers the same strict way.
	- A quote inside a quoted CSV field written as two quotes
	  ("Adeste ""Overture""") shows as one quote, not two.
	- A table whose file name has a capital letter (BandMusic.csv) can be
	  pivoted, charted and merged; only its own page opened.
	[ Other ]
	- Database::Abstraction 0.56; CGI::Lingua 0.88, which reads the locale
	  for the scripts' messages; Object::Configure 0.25; Statistics::LineFit
	  and Statistics::Lite.

0.021.0	Sat Nov  7 03:26:25 PM EST 2026
	[ Security ]
	- Optional login (password_hash, made by script/database-bi password),
	  with session expiry and lockout.
	- Listens on 127.0.0.1 by default; listening elsewhere, or answering
	  another machine, needs password_hash.
	- file_roots confines the folders readers may browse, open and export
	  to; export_dirs confines exports, and is required with a password.
	- Exports no longer write through a link, into a dot-folder or over a
	  non-file.
	- Cross-site writes and unexpected Host headers (DNS rebinding) are
	  refused; allowed_hosts adds names.
	- /import and url: sources refuse local and private-network addresses;
	  url_private_hosts allows intranet names.
	- Share and embed tokens are no longer signed with a fixed key.
	- /api/ask and /api/summary are bounded (ai_calls_per_hour, default 60).
	- Content-Security-Policy with script nonces, and the usual hardening
	  headers; embed_frame_ancestors allows framing of /embed/.
	- A setting that would not do what it says stops the start.
	[ Enhancements ]
	- .json files open like any other file: a list of objects, an object of
	  objects, or an object of such lists (one card each on /tables).
	- Print prints every row of the view, as the table shows it; past
	  10,000 rows it asks first.
	- Time range is offered only when the view has a date or time column.
	- "between" works on number columns too, and its bounds may be written
	  either way round.
	- path:<file>#<table> names one table of a file outside data_dir.
	- bi-extract, bi-geocode and bi-alert are installed, with manual pages.
	- Now required: Database::Abstraction 0.55, Database::Join 0.011.1
	  (DBI is no longer a runtime prerequisite), HTML::D3 0.30 and
	  Crypt::URandom.  File::Temp 0.2308 is no longer needed.
	[ Bug fixes ]
	- An unknown filter operator, or a filter or time range on a missing
	  column, is now a 400 that says so, instead of silently keeping every
	  row or none.
	- Timestamps ending in Z, an offset or a fraction of a second are read
	  as dates and times (as written; the zone is not applied).
	- A UTF-8 byte-order mark (Excel's "CSV UTF-8") is no longer part of
	  the first column's name.
	- Windows: file_roots and export_dirs work, including folders named
	  through a link; Perl 5.38 no longer hangs where a time is shown
	  (POSIX::strftime did not return there).
	- The BSDs: a folder where a store file should be is no longer moved
	  aside as corrupt.
	- bi-alert finds the modules when run from a source folder.
	- Tests no longer fail where paths are spelt differently (Windows,
	  macOS) or hang on Windows CI; a Platform paths workflow simulates
	  both on Linux.

0.020.0	Wed Nov  4 09:25:26 PM EST 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- The line graph panel has Lines by, which draws one line for each value of a
	  column (series=).  It was reachable only by writing the URL.
	- Canvas: Add current view offers Lines by for a line card.
	- /histogram takes scheme=, which fills the bars with the first colour of a
	  categorical scheme.  The histogram panel and Add current view offer it as
	  Bar colour, and a canvas histogram card keeps it.
	- Canvas: Add current view offers Colour by, Colour scheme, Bubble size and
	  the radii for a scatter card, as the scatter panel does.
	- /treemap takes color=, which shades each tile by a second column, with
	  color_agg= (default avg) and color_scheme= (sequential, default YlOrRd).
	  A legend shows the scale.  The treemap panel, Add current view and a
	  canvas treemap card all offer it.  It was a 400 before.
	- /combo takes scheme=: the bars take its first colour and the line its
	  second.  The combo panel, Add current view and the card offer it.
	- HTML::D3 0.29 is now required.  Besides the two above, it escapes the
	  text in the stacked, combo and treemap tooltips, where a label holding
	  markup ran when the chart was hovered.
	- Geocoding goes through a Geo::Coder::List chain: Geo::Coder::Free's
	  local data first, then OpenStreetMap's Nominatim (Geo::Coder::OSM) for
	  the places it does not know.  Requests are kept to one a second by
	  LWP::UserAgent::Throttled, from a client named Database-BI.  A map page
	  asks Nominatim about at most map_geocode_online_max places (default 20)
	  and leaves the rest for the next visit or script/bi-geocode.
	  map_geocode_online => 0 keeps every lookup local.  A Nominatim that
	  fails is not taken to mean the place does not exist: the place is
	  tried again later.  Geo::Coder::List, Geo::Coder::OSM and
	  LWP::UserAgent::Throttled are new recommends.
	- Geo::Coder::List keeps the answers it finds in a cache under
	  <model_dir>/geocode-cache, shared by /map and script/bi-geocode, so a
	  place Nominatim found is not asked again for a month.  The local data
	  is asked through the same cache, with or without Nominatim, and a place
	  whose local lookup fails is left for later rather than ending the
	  page.  Nominatim is asked over https, one request a lookup
	  (Geo::Coder::OSM asked over http, and was redirected).
	- /map takes loc= more than once, for a place written across columns:
	  loc=Address&loc=Country looks up "Brezje 8, Cerknica, Slovenia".  The
	  map panel has a Followed by column (guessed when one is called
	  Country, and remembered with the other), canvas map cards keep the
	  columns as a list, and script/bi-geocode takes --col more than once.
	- script/bi-geocode takes --offline and --online-max.
	- When a geocoded map cannot plot some rows, the note that says so opens
	  onto a list of their places: how many rows each stands for, why (no
	  place, not a place name, not found, or not looked up yet), and a link
	  to those rows in the table it came from.
	- The note that some rows of a map could not be plotted names only the
	  reasons that apply, each with its rows ("8 whose place was not found"),
	  rather than every reason there could be.  Beside it, one link opens
	  all those rows in the table at once.
	- A new filter operator, in, keeps the rows whose cell is any value of a
	  JSON list: f=place:in:["Paris","Rome"].  It is what that link uses.

	- With HTML::OSM 0.13, every map has a scale bar, and a map coloured by
	  series=, color= rules or size= has its key drawn on the map.  The key
	  lists only the colours of the markers in view, so it changes as the
	  map is zoomed and moved; bubble sizes are always listed.  A map with
	  no zoom= opens on every marker, as the map panel says; it used to open
	  at zoom 12 on the markers' midpoint, which could show none of them.
	  HTML::OSM 0.13 is now the recommended minimum: with an older one a map
	  page says which version it needs, as it did below 0.12.
	- A map no longer waits for its places to be looked up before it is
	  shown.  It draws what the place cache knows, and a progress bar names
	  each place as it is looked up ("Looking up 5 of 10: Brezje 8,
	  Cerknica, Slovenia"), with a Cancel button.  Each answer is saved as
	  it comes (POST /geo/lookup), so a run that is cancelled or interrupted
	  keeps what it found, and the map is drawn again when the last place is
	  in.  After Cancel the places left are listed as not looked up yet, with
	  a link to look them up.  The progress bar asks one place at a time, so
	  map_geocode_online_max no longer leaves places for the next visit, where
	  each paid for a slow local lookup again.  geocode=wait keeps the old
	  behaviour, which a client that is not a browser still gets;
	  geocode=cached looks nothing up.
	- The application's secret is random for each start unless the config
	  sets secrets => [...].  Mojolicious used the application's name, which
	  anyone can read; it now signs the places a map page lists.

	[ Bug fixes ]
	- A CSV whose first data row had a value in a column that is mostly
	  empty showed only the rows with a value there: a bank export whose
	  first transaction had a cheque number showed 4 of its 362 rows.  The
	  column rows are keyed on was the first one filled in the first row,
	  and Database::Abstraction drops a row whose key is empty.  It is now
	  the column with the fewest empty cells in the whole file, read as
	  Database::Abstraction reads it: a row such as "Joe\"s Bar" no longer
	  ends the count, and a line that will not parse is skipped.
	- DataSource read no rows when its caller had set $/ (slurp mode, or a
	  record length): the header was read as the whole file.  The methods
	  that read a file now use "\n" whatever the caller's $/ is.
	- On Windows a path: spec reached through a symlink was filed in the
	  model store under the link's name, not the file's, because realpath
	  there leaves links alone: definitions saved through the link were not
	  found from the file.  canonical_spec now follows the links first.
	- Deleting a canvas card rewrote a bookmark's cv= of more than nine
	  digits, which names no card, as cv=1e+20.  It is left alone, and a
	  search or card index that is not one is no longer renumbered.
	- A map marker's popup showed only the first of the rows at its place:
	  "3 rows" and one event.  It now lists them all, a value every row
	  shares once and the columns that differ as a small table.  Past
	  map_popup_rows (default 10) the rest are counted, with a link to them
	  in the table, and the table scrolls, so a busy place does not cover the
	  map.  The same holds for rows at one coordinate.
	- A map card on a canvas is coloured and sized as the map it was saved
	  from.  It stores color and size in its encode, which only the map page's
	  options were read for, so a card drew plain pins.
	- Calc::date_normalise and the date functions in calculated fields gave
	  the caller back a changed $!: on BSD, mktime and localtime can
	  leave errno set when they succeed (a CPAN Testers failure in t/unit.t).
	  Fixes https://www.cpantesters.org/cpan/report/ef94d1fc-d7aa-11f1-839c-f58cc80b69bd
	- script/bi-geocode could not open a path: spec: it looked for a table
	  named after the whole file name, extension included.
	- /scatter no longer saves scheme= in the chart it hands to a canvas when
	  series= is absent, where the scheme is ignored and unchecked.
	- A scatter added to a canvas from /scatter kept only x and y: the card now
	  colours by series and sizes bubbles as the page does, and the page saves
	  min_r= and max_r= with it.  A bad saved scheme draws the default colours.
	- A line chart added to a canvas from /graph?series= drew every row as one
	  line through all the groups.  The card now draws one line per value.
	- script/bi-geocode died on every run ('option "max" must be a positive
	  integer, not "0"'), and its summary counted cache hits from a key that
	  does not exist and compared rows with places.
	- A canvas map card whose saved color rules held something other than a
	  rule (PUT and import store a card as sent) was a 500; such entries are
	  now ignored.  A card whose encode, options or filters are not of the
	  right shape is drawn as if they were absent, rather than failing.
	- cv=abc on a canvas was read as card 0, which was then drawn unfiltered
	  as the source of the cross-filter (and perl warned).  Only a whole
	  number names a card.
	- Removing a text card left a bookmark's cv= naming the removed card,
	  which then named the card that moved into its place.  It is dropped.
	- A negative offset in a page of rows is read as 0, and a map's unplotted
	  rows ignore anything in the rows that is not a row.
	- A column-listing error on a table page lost only the last "at FILE line
	  N" of a rethrown error; it now goes through the same tidying as every
	  other error shown to a reader.
	[ Tests ]
	- t/edge_cases.t attacks the id choice (escaped quotes, unparseable and
	  stuck reads, a read failing mid-file, 50,000 rows, 2,000 columns, a
	  megabyte of quotes, the caller's $/, descriptor leaks and a full
	  descriptor table), bookmark renumbering, cv= values, stored cards of the
	  wrong shape and map popups (hostile cells, 100,000 rows at a marker).
	- t/function.t covers the geocoding progress run, the remaining
	  DataSource, Calc and Geocode helpers, and the Dashboard helpers and
	  canvas, lookup, extraction and /geo/lookup routes no part reached.
	- t/function.t tries _follow_links on an NT-form link target only where
	  readlink gives the target back as written: Cygwin turns a target with
	  backslashes into a Windows path (a CPAN Testers failure on Cygwin).
	  Fixes https://www.cpantesters.org/cpan/report/4d672bda-d7ac-11f1-8016-ac2316d9a04a
	- The geocoding tests pass without the recommended modules, as on CI:
	  t/transition.t stands in for Geo::Coder::List, t/unit.t raises the
	  "needs Geo::Coder::List" message with it hidden, and t/integration.t
	  counts the geocoder as installed only with Geo::Coder::List as well.
	- t/scatter.t: series=, scheme=, size=, min_r= and max_r= on /scatter and
	  on a canvas scatter card, and the scatter fields of both panels.
	- t/graph-series.t: series= on /graph, on a canvas line card and in both
	  panels.
	- t/histogram-scheme.t: scheme= on /histogram, on a canvas card and in both
	  panels.
	- t/geocode-online.t: the geocoding chain against a stand-in Nominatim
	  (t/lib/BI/Test/Nominatim.pm), so no test reaches the network.
	- t/treemap-combo-colour.t: color=, color_agg= and color_scheme= on
	  /treemap, including the Other tile; scheme= on /combo; both canvas cards
	  and all four panels.

0.019.0	Tue Nov  3 11:46:08 AM EST 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Stored lookups: POST /lookups/save adds the columns of another file under
	  data_dir to every matching row, on every page, chart, export and canvas.
	  nolk= skips one for a view; lk= applies one stored for another dataset.
	- Field extraction: rx() in a calculated field takes a value out of a column
	  with a regular expression, and POST /extract/save stores a pattern whose
	  named groups become columns everywhere.
	- Accelerated datasets: script/bi-extract builds a SQLite copy of a large
	  file under <model_dir>/extracts/, which the pages read while the file is
	  unchanged.  Once it changes, the file is read and the page says so.
	- A calculated field can read a cross-row column (cs=).
	- MariaDB and MySQL: mariadb:server/table opens a table of a server named in
	  mariadb_servers, anywhere a source is taken.  The spec holds only the name.
	- Export PDF on every chart page.
	- Canvas: text cards, for titles and explanations.  Cards save as SVG, PNG
	  and PDF, showing the whole chart and a pie's legend.
	- Saved charts carry "Database::BI" at the bottom right.
	- A website, in website/html/.
	- script/database-bi FILE starts the server showing that database.
	- Dark mode, chosen on a new Preferences page.  Saved charts, printed pages
	  and embedded canvases stay light.
	- Every feature can be reached from the pages: Lookups and Extract fields
	  panels, a canvas Share panel (link, embed code, JSON download) with Import
	  canvas to read it back, and Forget remembered columns on the map panel.
	- A refused request in a browser shows the reason on a page with a way back.
	- POST /geo/save takes repeated col=/kind= pairs, and replace=1.
	- Canvases in the main navigation opens a new /canvases page.
	- Database::Abstraction 0.54, File::Temp 0.2308 and HTML::D3 0.27 are now
	  required.
	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- Security: error replies that repeat a parameter were sent as HTML, so a
	  crafted link such as /bar?range=<script> ran script on this site.
	  scheme= reached a chart's script unchecked.  A cell holding
	  </script><script> ran on /bar, /pie, /stacked and /graph.
	- On Windows, a symlink was not followed when checking that a file is under
	  data_dir, so a link could point a lookup outside it.
	- Saved definitions for a path: dataset reached through a symlink were not
	  found by its /open page.
	- week() was 0 on Windows.
	- A saved definition that could no longer be applied made every page of its
	  dataset a 400, so the panel that mends it could not be reached.  The table
	  page is now drawn without it, with a note saying what is wrong.
	- A saved group's column was missing from the table page and canvas cards,
	  and groups saved from the panel never matched a row.
	- The map panel's Remember columns added to the saved columns rather than
	  replacing them.
	- A panel reopened after a save could list what was there before.
	- A SQLite .db file holding a table named after the file was read as text.
	- A SQLite file could not be opened when its first table's name had
	  characters other than letters, digits and underscores.
	- /open, /join and /combine left out calculated fields and span= time
	  buckets.  cs= on the table page could not read them.
	- A filter on a cross-row column, a calculated column or a time bucket found
	  no rows.
	- lt, le, gt and ge treated a blank or a word as zero; they now compare
	  numbers only, and read $1,234.56 and (1,234.56).
	- A column of codes (SKU123) or dates was read as numbers; 1e3 was read as 13.
	- Merge hung on BSDs with the File::Temp that Perl 5.28 ships.
	- hypnotoad script/database-bi never served the application.
	- Page links on a table opened with no other query were broken.
	- A file with no header row split quoted fields and dropped empty ones.
	  Sorting did nothing on such files, on XLSX and Berkeley DB, and on columns
	  such as "First Name".  XLSX and Berkeley DB are now read by
	  Database::Abstraction.
	- /api/range ignored joins and combines.
	- DataSource->updated() now gives the file's mtime.
	- /embed/:id drew no charts.  A canvas card's CSV download always failed.
	- Writes to analyses.json reported success when nothing was saved; they now
	  answer 503.  A store that could not be parsed was replaced by the next
	  save; it is now kept as <file>.corrupt.<time>.
	- A canvas or models.json of the wrong shape was a 500.  PUT, PATCH and
	  import now refuse or drop what POST does.
	- /export and /folder hung on a bad span= or calculated field.
	- 500s and exception pages now give a message instead: an unreadable data
	  or staging directory, SQLite export without a driver, a JSON URL that
	  holds no rows, a failing map renderer, a sorted export or a page on a
	  read-only backend, an unknown heatmap scheme.
	- Errors no longer show readers Perl source paths, line numbers or @INC.
	- An absolute data_dir defeated the check that keeps analyses.json out of it.
	- Non-numeric page=, per_page=, topn= and bins= are ignored.
	- Line chart exports cut off rotated labels, and saved charts lost their
	  fonts.
	- Smaller fixes in Insights, calculated fields, Summary, Ask, the map and
	  links built from non-ASCII text.
	- Tutorial: new chapters on navigation, Ask, bi-geocode and the settings
	  file, and the existing ones brought up to date.

0.018.0	Wed Oct 28 08:41:28 PM EDT 2026
	[ Enhancements ]
	- Cross-row columns: cs=<col>:<fn>:<alias>[:<arg>] with by= and order=
	  adds a running total, moving average, rank, z-score or share of the
	  total per row.  A Cross-row panel builds them; charts and exports
	  carry the new column.
	- Canvas cards can carry their own cross-row plan (cross_row), replacing
	  the page's, run on the card's own filtered rows.  Add current view
	  collects several columns; the card header's Edit cross-row edits the
	  plan as chips and warns when a rename affects a plotted column.
	  PATCH /api/analyses/:id/visuals/:visual replaces a card's plan.
	- Word cloud (GET /wordcloud, and canvas cards with kind=wordcloud),
	  drawn with HTML::TagCloud.  Each word links to its rows, or on a
	  canvas cross-filters to them.
	- Relative time ranges: ?range=<name>&rcol=<column> on every data route
	  and saved canvas (last_24_hours, today, yesterday, last_7_days,
	  last_30_days, last_90_days, this_month, last_month, this_quarter,
	  last_quarter, this_year, last_year).  An unknown name or missing rcol=
	  is a 400.  The dashboard filter bar and canvas toolbar have a Time
	  range menu.
	- The filter UI adapts to the column's detected type: date pickers,
	  numeric operators, or string operators.
	- New "between" filter operator (col:between:YYYY-MM-DD:YYYY-MM-DD);
	  lt/le/gt/ge compare ISO dates as dates.
	- New measures for the pivot, column statistics and canvas cards:
	  median, stdev, first, last, range and p<N> percentiles.
	- Time buckets: span= groups a chart or pivot by hour, day, week, month,
	  quarter or year; /graph?agg= plots one point per bucket.
	- Stored time patterns accept time=, e.g. 27/10/2026 14:05.
	- bi-alert: --suppress, --recovery (exit 3), --notify-cmd, and several
	  analyses and thresholds in one run.
	- Response compression, ETag/304 and HTML minification via
	  Mojolicious::Plugin::Buffer (new prerequisite); downloads opt out.
	- Optional page cache: page_cache_dir and page_cache_duration store
	  /catalog and /tutorial in a CHI File cache.
	- Internal: every data route's filter stage is one function,
	  _reduce_records.
	- Main navigation links to Canvases, Catalog and Open file on every page.
	- Chart, pivot, stats and insights pages show a "Drawn from:" strip naming
	  the time range, filters, cross-row columns, combined files and
	  de-duplication, with a link back to change them.
	- Every page has a full breadcrumb, from Home to the page itself, each
	  step named after what it shows: Home > sales > Bar chart: amount by
	  region > sales (region = East) > Pie chart: Count by region.  The
	  trail is read from each page's back links, so it is no longer cut at
	  two levels; a file opened from the browser sits under its folder, and
	  a canvas is named.  Back links to another site are not followed, and
	  the trail is at most eight steps.
	[ Bug Fixes ]
	- Two charts on one canvas no longer collide; the second card draws.
	- A card's cross-row column no longer appears in the cards beside it.
	- A card naming a column no plan produces is refused, instead of
	  printing a heading over empty cells.
	- A day bound on a timestamp column (e.g. le 2026-10-27) now includes
	  the rows stamped on that day.
	- A stale ?dd= bookmark no longer makes canvas marks unfocusable.
	- Canvas help now describes what the page actually does.
	- Charts, pivots, stats and insights of a combined (c=), de-duplicated
	  (d=1) or inner/outer-joined (jtype=) view now draw from the rows the
	  table shows; before, the panels dropped those parameters, and d=1 was
	  ignored by every chart route.  Export keeps jtype= too.
	- Opening any toolbar panel closes all the others: the histogram panel
	  left Cross-row and Word cloud open, and Merge, Combine and Export left
	  chart panels open and their own buttons disabled.
	- The Word cloud, Cross-row and Ask toolbar buttons are styled like the
	  others; all of them now share one btn-tool class.
	- Toolbar and filter-bar controls on the same row now line up top and
	  bottom: they share one height and are centred, rather than aligned on
	  their text baselines.
	- The pivot panel's Limit to top, ranked by, Time bucket and "of column"
	  fields are the same size as its Row, Column and Measure fields.
	- Canvas controls line up: the Time range bar, parameter bar and toolbar
	  share the table toolbar's height, the Time range menus and Apply
	  button are styled, and card-header buttons are one height.
	- Every dropdown and text field is white with one border colour, from a
	  single rule in the layout; the table page's filter bar and pivot fields
	  showed the browser's grey, and pivot fields turned near-black when the
	  OS was in dark mode.
	- An unknown canvas (/a/<id>) is an HTML page with the breadcrumb and
	  navigation, not a line of plain text.
	- Folder view levels keep the link back to the table they came from,
	  and its crumb is an absolute link.
	- A missing page is a 404 with the breadcrumb and navigation, not
	  Mojolicious's bare page.  /view/<table> for a table that does not
	  exist is now a 404 naming it, rather than the home page with an
	  error that showed the data directory.
	- A view that is both joined (j=) and combined (c=) shows both, from
	  /join or /combine: /combine ignored j=, and Merge after Combine
	  dropped c=.  The table now shows the rows its charts draw from.
	- Tests: the read-only model_dir and export-write-failure tests skip
	  where chmod cannot revoke access (Cygwin, Windows ACLs, root), using
	  Test::Permissions; they failed on Cygwin (CPAN Testers).
	  Fixes https://www.cpantesters.org/cpan/report/b2c11820-d297-11f1-a488-efeb21b3859a
